---
import { getEntry, render } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { LAYERS, QUESTIONS, CELLS, VOCABULARY, FRAMEWORK_VERSION } from '../data/framework';

const entry = await getEntry('framework', 'framework');
if (!entry) throw new Error('src/content/framework/framework.md is missing');
const { Content } = await render(entry);

// The vocabulary as a DefinedTermSet: what makes the framework's terms citable
// as terms rather than as prose. See ADR-COM-0004.
const site = Astro.site?.href.replace(/\/$/, '') ?? 'https://contentoperatingmodel.com';
const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@type': 'DefinedTermSet',
    '@id': `${site}/framework#vocabulary`,
    name: 'Content Operating Model vocabulary',
    version: FRAMEWORK_VERSION,
    hasDefinedTerm: VOCABULARY.filter((term) => !term.rejected).map((term) => ({
      '@type': 'DefinedTerm',
      name: term.term,
      description: term.definition,
      inDefinedTermSet: `${site}/framework#vocabulary`,
    })),
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title={entry.data.title}
  metaTitle={entry.data.metaTitle}
  description={entry.data.description}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  markdownTwin="/framework.md"
>
  <article class="c-marketing">
    <section class="c-marketing-section">
      <div class="c-section__inner">
        <p class="c-eyebrow">The framework</p>
        <h1 class="display-heading type-h1">{entry.data.title}</h1>
        <p class="type-intro">
          Version {FRAMEWORK_VERSION} · September 2026 · an open framework from
          Contentious, being tested with real organisations before 1.0.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="c-marketing-section prose">
      <div class="c-section__inner"><Content /></div>
    </section>

    <section class="c-marketing-section c-marketing-section--tint">
      <div class="c-section__inner">
        <p class="c-eyebrow">The structure</p>
        <h2 id="the-three-layers" class="display-heading type-h2">The three layers</h2>
        <p>The model describes three layers of organisational material, stacked.</p>
        <div class="stack">
          {LAYERS.map((layer) => (
            <div class={`layer${layer.foundation ? '' : ' layer--top'}`}>
              <p class="c-eyebrow">{layer.foundation ? 'Foundation' : 'The working machinery'}</p>
              <h3 id={layer.id}>{layer.name}</h3>
              <p>{layer.summary}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p>
          Content is the only layer the audience sees, which is why organisations
          over-invest in it and under-invest beneath it. Most content problems
          that look like writing problems are layer problems: the words are fine,
          and there is nothing under them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="c-marketing-section">
      <div class="c-section__inner">
        <p class="c-eyebrow">The content layer</p>
        <h2 id="the-seven-questions" class="display-heading type-h2">The seven questions</h2>
        <p>
          A working model answers seven questions about how the layers become
          published content. Why sits at the centre, and the other six connect to
          it, because none of them can be answered well until it is.
        </p>
        <dl>
          {QUESTIONS.map((question) => (
            <div id={question.id}>
              <dt class="display-heading">{question.name} <span class="type-sm">{question.subtitle}</span></dt>
              <dd>{question.summary}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="c-marketing-section c-marketing-section--tint">
      <div class="c-section__inner">
        <p class="c-eyebrow">Two registers</p>
        <h2 id="rule-and-call" class="display-heading type-h2">Every question is answered twice</h2>
        <p>
          A strategy sets the rule once; an operating model makes the call every
          time. Rules without calls give you the beautiful strategy deck and the
          chaotic Tuesdays. Calls without rules give you the opposite
          organisation: the smooth machine whose workflow hums and whose content
          nobody can explain the point of.
        </p>
        <p>
          AI makes this split impossible to ignore, because AI only accelerates
          one side of it. Ask an agent to draft forty pages and it will make
          hundreds of calls before lunch. What it cannot do is supply the rules it
          is making them against.
        </p>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Question</th>
              <th scope="col">The rule, set once</th>
              <th scope="col">The call, made every time</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {QUESTIONS.map((question) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{question.name}</th>
                <td>{question.rule}</td>
                <td>{question.call}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="type-sm">
          Crossing the seven questions with the two registers gives the model its
          working surface: {CELLS.length} cells, each of them concrete, each
          auditable. The 1.0 toolkit will provide templates for all of them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="c-marketing-section">
      <div class="c-section__inner">
        <p class="c-eyebrow">Vocabulary</p>
        <h2 id="vocabulary" class="display-heading type-h2">The words, and what they mean</h2>
        <dl>
          {VOCABULARY.filter((term) => !term.rejected).map((term) => (
            <div>
              <dt class="display-heading">{term.term}</dt>
              <dd>{term.definition}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
        <h3 id="terms-not-used">Terms deliberately not used</h3>
        <dl>
          {VOCABULARY.filter((term) => term.rejected).map((term) => (
            <div>
              <dt class="display-heading">{term.term}</dt>
              <dd>{term.definition}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="c-marketing-section c-marketing-section--tint">
      <div class="c-section__inner">
        <p class="c-eyebrow">Versioning</p>
        <h2 id="version" class="display-heading type-h2">Version {FRAMEWORK_VERSION}, and what happens next</h2>
        <p>
          This page carries a version number because the framework practises what
          it preaches. Changes are logged, release by release, in
          <a href="/changelog">the changelog</a>, and nothing gets called
          canonical until teams that do not work for us have used it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
